# Docket — E-Commerce Order & Delivery Management Console

A pure **HTML / CSS / vanilla JavaScript (ES Modules)** implementation of the
OOP assignment. No build step, no backend, no npm install — open it and it
runs, including directly on **GitHub Pages**.

---

## 1. Folder structure — where everything lives

```
ecommerce-oop/
├── index.html              ← the only HTML page (single-page app)
├── css/
│   └── style.css            ← all styling, mobile-responsive
└── js/
    ├── app.js                ← DOM/UI layer only — wires buttons to models
    └── models/                ← ALL the OOP logic. No DOM code in here.
        ├── User.js             (User, Admin)
        ├── Product.js          (Product, PhysicalProduct, DigitalProduct, SubscriptionProduct)
        ├── Customer.js         (Customer, RegularCustomer, PremiumCustomer, BusinessCustomer)
        ├── PaymentMethod.js    (PaymentMethod, CreditCard, BankTransfer, DigitalWallet)
        ├── DeliveryMethod.js   (DeliveryMethod, StandardDelivery, ExpressDelivery, SameDayDelivery)
        ├── Discount.js         (Discount, PercentageDiscount, FixedAmountDiscount, BuyOneGetOneDiscount, SeasonalDiscount)
        ├── OrderItem.js
        ├── Order.js            (the lifecycle state machine + total calculation)
        ├── ShoppingCart.js
        ├── Invoice.js
        ├── Refund.js
        └── Store.js
```

**Rule of thumb:** business logic → `js/models/*.js`. Screen rendering and
click handlers → `js/app.js`. `index.html` never contains logic, only markup.

---

## 2. How to run it

### Option A — GitHub Pages (recommended, zero setup)
1. Create a new GitHub repo and push the **contents of this folder** to the
   repo root (so `index.html` sits at the repo root, not inside a subfolder).
2. Repo → **Settings → Pages → Build and deployment → Source: "Deploy from a
   branch"** → Branch: `main`, folder: `/ (root)` → **Save**.
3. Wait ~1 minute, then open the URL GitHub gives you
   (`https://<username>.github.io/<repo-name>/`).

Because every file reference in this project is a **relative path**
(`css/style.css`, `js/app.js`, `./models/...`) and every filename matches its
`import` statement **exactly in case**, this deploys with no 404s regardless
of the repository name.

### Option B — Local preview
Browsers block ES module imports on the `file://` protocol (CORS), so
double-clicking `index.html` will show a blank page in some browsers. Serve
it locally instead:

```bash
# any of these work — pick whichever you have installed
npx serve .
python3 -m http.server 5500
php -S localhost:5500
```

Then open `http://localhost:5500`.

> This restriction only affects local testing. GitHub Pages serves over
> HTTPS, so modules load correctly there with no extra steps.

---

## 3. What the app actually does

It's a working, in-memory storefront:

- **Catalog** — 7 seeded products across the three product classes, filterable by type.
- **Cart & Checkout** — live price breakdown (subtotal, shipping, tax,
  customer discount, promo discount, total) that recalculates on every click
  by calling the real model methods — nothing here is hard-coded.
- **Payment & delivery** — pick a payment method and a delivery method; the
  cost/timeline shown is computed by that concrete class's own method.
- **Orders** — every order shows a live lifecycle rail (`CREATED → CONFIRMED
  → PAID → PROCESSING → SHIPPED → DELIVERED`, or `CANCELLED` / `REFUNDED`)
  and action buttons that only appear when the transition is legal. Try
  "Try cancel (blocked)" on a delivered order — the **System Log** panel on
  the right shows the model rejecting it in real time.
- **System Log** — a live terminal feed of every business-rule success/
  failure, so grading/reviewing the enforced rules doesn't require opening
  devtools.
- **Class Map tab** — a quick on-page reference of the class hierarchy for
  anyone (e.g. an instructor) skimming the app without reading the source.

Switch the "Signed in as" customer in the top bar to see
`RegularCustomer` / `PremiumCustomer` / `BusinessCustomer` change the
discount and free-shipping outcome on the exact same cart — that's
polymorphism, visible without reading code.

---

## 4. How each requirement is demonstrated

| Requirement | Where |
|---|---|
| **Encapsulation** | Every class uses `#privateFields`. `Order#status`, `#items`, `#totalAmount`-equivalent are never assigned from outside — only `addItem()`, `removeItem()`, `confirm()`, `pay()`, `process()`, `ship()`, `deliver()`, `cancel()`, `refund()` in `Order.js` can change them, and each enforces its own rule (stock can't go negative in `Product.js`, wallet can't go negative in `Customer.js`, an invoice can't be refunded twice in `Invoice.js`). |
| **Abstraction** | `Product`, `Customer`, `PaymentMethod`, `DeliveryMethod`, and `Discount` are all base classes that throw if instantiated directly (`new.target === X` guard) and declare methods that subclasses must implement. `Order.js` only ever calls the abstract method names (`calculatePrice`, `calculateDiscount`, `pay`, `calculateShippingCost`, `applyDiscount`) — never a concrete class name. |
| **Inheritance** | `Product → PhysicalProduct/DigitalProduct/SubscriptionProduct`, `Customer → Regular/Premium/Business`, `PaymentMethod → CreditCard/BankTransfer/DigitalWallet`, `DeliveryMethod → Standard/Express/SameDay`, `Discount → Percentage/FixedAmount/BuyOneGetOne/Seasonal`, `User → Admin/Customer`. Shared fields/behavior live once in the base class. |
| **Polymorphism** | `Order.calculateTotal()` calls `item.lineTotal()` → `product.calculatePrice()`, `customer.calculateDiscount()`, `deliveryMethod.calculateShippingCost()`, and sums `discount.applyDiscount()` over an array of *mixed* discount types — with **zero** `if (type === ...)` branching anywhere in `Order.js`, `Product.js`, `Customer.js`, `PaymentMethod.js`, or `Discount.js`. `app.js` does use `instanceof` twice, but only to choose which **extra UI fields** to display (e.g. show stock vs. show a download link) — never to decide pricing, discount, shipping, or lifecycle behavior. |
| **Composition** | `Order` owns `OrderItem[]` (`OrderItem.js` has no independent lifecycle). |
| **Association** | `Customer` places `Order`s (`Order` holds a reference to `Customer`, but a `Customer` can exist without ever placing one). |
| **Aggregation** | `Store` holds `Product[]` (`Store.js`), but a `Product` object is still meaningfully referenced by `OrderItem`s and cart lines even if the `Store` "removes" it from its own catalog. |
| **Order calculation** | `Order.calculateTotal()` in `Order.js`: `subtotal + shipping + tax - customerDiscount - promoDiscount`, clamped at 0 — split across `productSubtotal()`, `shippingCost()`, `taxAmount()`, `customerDiscountAmount()`, `promoDiscountAmount()`, not one giant method. |
| **Order lifecycle** | `Order.js` keeps a `TRANSITIONS` table and an internal `#assertTransition()` guard — illegal moves (e.g. `DELIVERED → CANCELLED`) throw before any state changes. |

---

## 5. Notes for the intern (you) presenting this

- All data is **in-memory only** — refreshing the page resets everything.
  That's intentional for an assignment demo; swapping in `localStorage` or a
  real backend would only touch `Store.js`/`app.js`, not the model classes.
- `Admin` (in `User.js`) is implemented to satisfy the required class list
  but isn't wired into the UI, since the brief's UI-facing flows are all
  customer-facing.
- Tax is a flat 10.5% to match the worked numeric example in the brief
  (₹/$10,000 → 1,050 tax). Change `TAX_RATE` at the top of `Order.js` if you
  need a different rate.